Overview of the Python & Playwright automation test framework

    Focus on two main folders in the source: src and TestSuites.

    Inside src, there are key subfolders. Read them carefully and follow the current coding style exactly—do not deviate.

    The src/Core folder is the heart of the framework. It contains prebuilt functions to use in automation scripts:

        For browser-related scripts, study every file under src/Core/page and use the exact function names found in:

        actions.py, assertions.py, browser.py, waits.py.

        Read all code in each file to understand and use them correctly.
        If any required function is missing, implement it following the current style, ensuring absolute accuracy—no omissions, no duplicates, and no multiple names for the same functionality.

    For API automation, read the files under src/Core/api/ and use functions correctly from:

        assertions.py, client.py, extractor.py.

    For database automation, read all files under the database package to use the functions precisely:

        connection.py, data.py, query.py.

    Finally, review utilities under src/Core/utilities—primarily file.py, assertions.py—for data/file comparisons and related helpers. Understand every function in detail.

The src/Interfaces folder stores element locator files (per page or sub-function) using XPath, CSS selectors, etc. Review these to understand the locator file style and follow it when implementing new elements.

The src/DataTest folder stores data files used by scripts. Each file maps to a page—review and implement according to the framework’s current style.

The src/Action folder is where business logic for automation is implemented. Use the corresponding files in src/Core, src/Interfaces, and src/DataTest to build accurate scripts for each test case.

The TestSuites folder imports all Actions for execution.
Important: Files in TestSuites are for running only—they must contain no business logic.

The framework already has setup and teardown. Follow the existing implementation style—do not introduce your own approach.

