    CRITICAL - Element Capture & Verification Rules:
    WARNING: ACCURATE ELEMENT = ACCURATE AUTOMATION - Wrong element will cause entire automation to fail!

    + ALWAYS use browser_get_page_html to get the complete page structure before capturing any element
    + MANDATORY: Use browser_evaluate with JavaScript to inspect elements precisely:
        - Access elements inside iframes (iframe.contentDocument)
        - Verify EXACT attribute values (className, id, name)
        - Test XPath/CSS selectors before implementing
        - Count matching elements to ensure uniqueness (MUST be exactly 1)
        - Check element visibility and state
    + After getting HTML, perform THOROUGH inspection following this checklist:

    1. CHECK FOR IFRAMES:
        - Scan the HTML for <iframe> tags
        - If element is inside iframe, you MUST handle iframe separately
        - Note the iframe selector and the element selector inside it
        - Test that the element is accessible within the correct iframe context

    2. CHECK FOR SHADOW DOM:
        - Look for shadow-root or elements with shadowRoot
        - If element is in shadow DOM, use special Playwright shadow DOM selectors
        - Verify shadow DOM accessibility before proceeding

    3. VERIFY ELEMENT UNIQUENESS:
        - Check if your selector returns ONLY ONE element
        - If multiple elements match, refine the selector to be more specific
        - Use unique attributes (id, data-testid, unique class combinations)
        - Avoid brittle selectors that may break with minor UI changes

    4. CHECK ELEMENT STABILITY:
        - Verify element is not dynamically loaded/removed
        - If dynamic, identify proper wait conditions (visible, attached, stable)
        - Note any loading states or animations that affect element availability

    5. LOCATOR STRATEGY PRIORITY (follow this order):
        a) data-testid or data-test attributes (most stable)
        b) Unique ID attributes
        c) Unique name attributes
        d) Unique combination of attributes
        e) Text content (for buttons, links with static text)
        f) CSS selectors (be specific)
        g) XPath (last resort - most brittle)

    6. MANDATORY VERIFICATION BEFORE SAVING:
        - Use browser_click or browser_type to TEST the element selector
        - Confirm the selector works in the actual browser context
        - If test fails, refine the selector and test again
        - NEVER save an element selector without testing it first

    7. DOCUMENT SPECIAL CASES:
        - If element requires wait conditions, note it in the element file
        - If element is in iframe, clearly document the iframe context
        - If element has multiple states (enabled/disabled), capture all relevant selectors

    + WORKFLOW using browser_evaluate for MAXIMUM ACCURACY:
        1. browser_navigate → Navigate to the page
        2. browser_snapshot → Get overview
        3. browser_get_page_html → Get HTML structure
        4. browser_evaluate → Inspect element PRECISELY with JavaScript:

           // Template for iframe element:
           (function() {
             const iframe = document.querySelector('iframe[name="FunctionFrame"]');
             const doc = iframe?.contentDocument || document;
             const element = doc.querySelector('YOUR_SELECTOR');
             return {
               exists: !!element,
               className: element?.className,
               id: element?.id,
               attributes: element ? Array.from(element.attributes).map(attr => ({name: attr.name, value: attr.value})) : [],
               outerHTML: element?.outerHTML.substring(0, 500)
             };
           })();

           // Template to verify XPath uniqueness:
           (function() {
             const iframe = document.querySelector('iframe[name="FunctionFrame"]');
             const doc = iframe?.contentDocument || document;
             const result = doc.evaluate("YOUR_XPATH", doc, null, XPathResult.ORDERED_NODE_SNAPSHOT_TYPE, null);
             return {
               count: result.snapshotLength,
               isUnique: result.snapshotLength === 1,
               elements: Array.from({length: result.snapshotLength}, (_, i) => ({
                 className: result.snapshotItem(i).className,
                 id: result.snapshotItem(i).id
               }))
             };
           })();

        5. Based on results → Create precise XPath/CSS selector
        6. Verify selector returns EXACTLY ONE element (isUnique: true)
        7. Test with browser_click/browser_type in real context
        8. Save to Interface file → Only after 100% verification

    + WHY browser_evaluate is CRITICAL:
        - Shows EXACT attribute values (not guessed from HTML)
        - Accesses iframe elements properly
        - Tests XPath before implementing
        - Counts elements to ensure uniqueness
        - More accurate than browser_get_page_html alone

    + REMEMBER: 5 minutes with browser_evaluate saves 5 hours debugging!
    + GOLDEN RULE: If you can't verify it with browser_evaluate, don't save it to Interface files!

    IMPORTANT - POM Element File Management Rules:
    + Each page MUST have only ONE interface file following the naming pattern: interface_{page_name}.py
    + Before creating any new interface file, ALWAYS check if a file for that page already exists in src/Interfaces/
    + If the interface file for a page already exists:
        - DO NOT create a new file with the same or similar name
        - ALWAYS add new elements to the existing file
        - Read the existing file first to check if the element already exists
        - Only add the element if it doesn't exist yet in that file
    + If the interface file does not exist for a new page:
        - Create a new file following the exact naming convention: interface_{page_name}.py
        - Follow the existing code style and structure from other interface files
    + Example: For "My Account" page, use interface_my_account.py (not interface_my_account_v2.py, not interface_myaccount_new.py)
    + This ensures clean POM architecture: one page = one interface file = all elements for that page in one place

